  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT SUN AUG 9 2026\n\nA 596 decameter subtropical ridge will strengthen over Florida\nand the eastern Gulf today. The slight increase in heights and\nflow aloft becoming a bit more anticyclonic may result in a\ndecrease in convective coverage especially the further south you\ngo. However, there will be little air mass change with PWATs in\nexcess of 2 inches, and wet ground conditions remain present.\nAdditionally, a weak shear axis will remain located along the\nTN/AL border which could serve as a focus for convective\ndevelopment eventually propagating southward on outflow\nboundaries. Therefore, we expect a persistence forecast and have\nincreased PoPs above NBM, in line with the latest HREF calibrated\nthunder probabilities. With the continued very unstable, high\nPWAT air mass, locally heavy rainfall will continue to be a\nthreat as well as isolated flash flooding primarily in urban\nareas. In addition, expect frequent lightning and gusty winds,\nwith a continued risk for isolated instances of damaging winds.\nHeat indices will approach 105 degrees in West Alabama ahead of\nany convection, but coverage and duration are expected to be too\nlimited to warrant a heat advisory. Tuesday and Wednesday the\nridge will slowly drift westward along the Gulf Coast, placing\nCentral Alabama under weak northwest flow aloft. Expect a similar\nstormy pattern to continue with heat indices once again\napproaching advisory criteria.\n\nThursday through the weekend, the ridge strengthens and builds\nnorthward over the Gulf Coast states. This should result in a\ndecrease in convective coverage, but low probabilities will\nremain each day given the hot and humid conditions. Heat indices\nof 105-110 should become more widespread resulting in medium to\nhigh (50-80%) chances for heat advisories. HeatRisk will increase\ninto the Major category across much of the area, with limited\nrecovery from the heat overnight.\n\n32/JDavis",
